\section{Introduction}\label{sec:ethics-intro}

Programming language design is not merely a technical activity. Every design decision---what to make easy, what to make hard, what to allow, what to forbid---has consequences for the people who use the language, the systems they build, and the society those systems affect. A language that makes it easy to write unsafe code enables security vulnerabilities at scale. A language that is inaccessible to non-experts reinforces existing inequalities. A language that encourages wasteful computation contributes to environmental degradation.

This chapter examines the ethical dimensions of language design. We consider the tension between safety and freedom, the responsibilities of language designers to their users, the environmental impact of programming languages, the governance of open-source language projects, and the broader social implications of the languages we create.

\section{Language Design as Social Engineering}\label{sec:social-engineering}

\subsection{Languages Shape Thought}

The Sapir--Whorf hypothesis, controversial in linguistics, has a clear analog in programming: the language we program in shapes how we think about computation. A language with first-class functions encourages functional thinking. A language with objects encourages thinking in terms of entities and messages. A language with dependent types encourages thinking in terms of proofs and specifications.

\begin{insight}
Language design is a form of social engineering: by choosing what to make easy and what to make hard, the language designer shapes the behavior of millions of programmers and the billions of people affected by the systems they build.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Defaults Matter}

The defaults of a language---what happens when the programmer doesn't specify---are among the most consequential design decisions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Memory safety}: C defaults to unsafe memory access; Rust defaults to safe access. The consequence: C programs have buffer overflows; Rust programs do not.
  \item \textbf{Null}: Java defaults to nullable references; Kotlin defaults to non-nullable. The consequence: Java programs have NullPointerExceptions; Kotlin programs do not.
  \item \textbf{Concurrency}: C defaults to shared mutable state; Erlang defaults to isolated processes. The consequence: C programs have data races; Erlang programs do not.
  \item \textbf{Integer overflow}: Java defaults to wrapping; Swift defaults to trapping. The consequence: Java programs have silent overflow bugs; Swift programs crash loudly.
\end{itemize}

Each default reflects a value judgment: is it more important to be fast or safe? To be flexible or predictable? To trust the programmer or protect them?

