For large projects compiled frequently (e.g., in CI/CD pipelines), the energy cost of compilation can be significant. Language designers should consider compilation energy as part of the overall environmental impact of their languages.

\section{Conclusion: Toward Responsible Language Design}\label{sec:ethics-conclusion}

The ethical dimensions of language design are often overlooked in favor of technical considerations. But every design decision has ethical implications: who benefits, who is harmed, what values are promoted, what risks are created.

The responsible language designer must:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Acknowledge the ethical dimensions of their work.
  \item Consider the impact on all stakeholders, not just the immediate users.
  \item Make trade-offs explicit and subject to community scrutiny.
  \item Build inclusive, well-governed communities.
  \item Consider the long-term consequences of their designs.
  \item Use their technical skills in the service of the common good.
\end{enumerate}

Language design is a powerful activity. The languages we create shape the software that runs the world. With that power comes responsibility---to our users, to our communities, to society, and to future generations.
